he data on that  and it's really hard to find that data  because the definitions are so variable  I think what's more important is to just  look at the negative effects of it and  see how common they are without the  labels even even the terms that we use  to label clinical prediction of  conditions like ADHD and autism are  often coming under stone challenge in  that the labeling process itself is is  imprecise let's now talk about sort of  how this relates to the way human brains  are sort of wired or inherently based on  our history in in Prior times before we  had industrialization modern technology  sort of modern Western culture talked a  little bit about the concept of  multitasking and the ways in which the  brain sort of reacts to moving from one  activity to another and maybe even how  that sort of served us in earlier times  in human history yes so the human brain  is it's amazing obviously I don't have  to convince anyone of that I would say  the most remarkable aspect of the human  brain is our goal-setting abilities  these very long time delayed complex  interweaving goals and whatever goals as  well with other people's goals but we  have these fundamental limitations and  how we enact those goals we call those  the abilities that allow us to do that  cognitive control our attention  abilities where we direct our limited  resources where and when we want them  holding that information in mind for  short periods of time something we call  working memory and then hope by tasking  and we are capable of doing this in  terms of our behavior but what we see  when we look at someone's brain with  functional neuroimaging as they attempt  to engage in or the one intentionally  demanding a task is that the brain  network that is activated to do one task  disengages at a new net  work is engaged we have a secondary task  what we don't see is parallel processing  of two tasks that you're engaged in it  both demand attention and so this is  just a fundamental limitation of all  brands and we see it across the app you  know all other animals and humans like  so we're the distracted mind comes in is  a is the conflict between our goals what  we want to do what were actually capable  of doing and although other animals have  these same limitations on multitasking  they don't really attempt to engage in  the environment in that way so they  don't have that same problem so it  sounds sort of like this is both a  nature and nurture thing in the sense  that when one might think that they're  multitasking really what they're doing  is switching sometimes rapidly from  focusing for a short period of time on  one task to focusing on another one  there must be some time costs in terms  of that switching that takes place but  is there also somewhat of a is this a  muscle of sorts in other words can the  sort of brain activity that is  encouraged by modern technology  smartphones etc can it put our brains  sort of out of practice of the habit of  deeper and longer focus on one task sure  so it was a lot in the back in a little  bit I mean nature nurture of course most  everything is the nation of our genetics  and environmental influences and this is  a very very nice example of that the  cost that you talk about is what we pay  a lot attention to as scientists and  it's people that are interested in  interference effects whether it be some  distraction or multi tasking or a phone  ringing around really exactly this fits  checked in mind for you in real world  what we see you know is a cost in both  response time as you described but also  in accuracy just the fidelity of  information that you're holding in mind  is degraded when interrupted as I just  was by another task and interfering  stimulus the question of plasticity can  we build up this ability so let's listen  in both directions it doesn't really  have a morality it takes you wherever  the influence is it modifies and  responds so if you spend your time  constantly switching whether you're  having a conversation you jump on  Twitter you move to your email you're  listening to music if you engage in that  way then it's very reasonable that other  skills that you're not practicing such  as sustained focus attention will become  weaker and each of you retrained what  are some good just basic practices for  the average person who maybe has a job  that depends pretty significantly on  being on technology devices internet  email etc and who wants to sort of enact  some protections to what can be this  natural slide to this regular switching  between tasks and scrolling on social  media feeds not ideas that will  basically take them out of the world  that they're in by virtue of being in  modern Western society but just sort of  day to day things everybody could do  yeah it's a great question I always like  to frame is that I love technology a lot  a lot I use the same tools that most  everyone has maybe even more and I don't  think this is a black or white issue I  don't think technology is good or bad  it's just that based on a better  understanding of where the weaknesses  and how our brain processes information  you can make an informed decision about  how you engage in technology which is  really the core of your question so I  would say if you recognize the  limitations and feel them and  acknowledge them and and how broad they  are how they impact not just your work  or your schoolwork but your social  interactions how they might impact your  safety if you're texting and driving  once you acknowledge them then you get  to make decisions and one of the  decisions that I make is that when I'm  engaged in something to really demand a  high level of quality especially if it's  time pressure associated with it I shut  off my other technological streams you  know I quit my email I shut my other  browsers if I'm working on one I close  my door I turn my plant  my phone to airplane mode as I should've  today and I really work by focusing on  that 